result.summary:
Rung O stopped because its count is 11, and I rule that the stop holds: a nonzero count is Pavol's decision (POSITIONS.md:88), and the batch record says a judge cannot lift it (CLIMB-BATCH-4.md:145). No continuation is written.
All 11 tests go from passing to an uncaught IntegerOverflow. The cause is code written in 2012 that wraps on purpose: four library files (range split, the QuickCheck random generator, ChunkedSparseArray, IntMap) and five of the team's tests.
The worker's count, method and site list check out against the source. Four things in the report are wrong or missing:
- keeping walk wrapping only postpones the work, because the compiled path throws and will compile the same library at the switch-over;
- the multiplication glyph should be ⨰, spelled DOTTIMES;
- the team's 2011 prelude already declares a wrapping operator set on every integer type, and the report missed that precedent;
- the library's reliance on wrapping deserves its own ledger row.
My recommendation: a library rung first declares the specification's ∔ ∸ ⨰ and moves the wrap-reliant code onto them, which changes no output; then rung O lands as decided, with its count re-measured.
The ruling is explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-overflow/JUDGE.md, committed as ff92e1cf1 on wip/rung-walk-overflow and pushed.

result.ruling:
The skeptic is right on the refusal ground. At Coercions.java:200-206 (the skeptic cites :212-217, but the method is at :200-206), CoercedCall applies the overload chosen before the conversion to the converted arguments. OverloadedMethod.java:58-62 does the same for methods. So g(NarrowOf(2)), with g(Wide) and g(WideOf) declared, gives g(Wide) under walk (probes/skeptic/diff-1.txt:5, :12), while the compiled run gives g(WideOf) (:21, :28). Walk's own bound value also gives g(WideOf) (:6), and so does walk's functional-method refinement inside the same CoercedCall (:8). The method twin behaves the same way (diff-3.txt:4, :9 against :16, :21).

The worker reads 'the declaration with parameter type T is applied to the call' (conversions-coercions.tex:535-536) as fixing the declaration that runs. That reading is wrong. The passage describes a static call, rewritten 'for pedagogical purposes; implementation techniques may vary' (:512-514); the rewriting inserts explicit coerce calls into the expression (:253-265). What :567-570 fixes statically is the coercion, not the declaration that runs. The overloading chapter says what runs: the statically most specific declaration is the anchor, and run-time dispatch considers 'that declaration plus declarations that are more specific than that declaration' (advanced/overloading.tex:73-78). 'The most specific declaration that is applicable to a dynamic call is more specific than' the static one (:466-468). Every call dispatches among the declarations applicable at run time (basic/overloading.tex:263-276). The specification's own example (:572-604) cannot tell the two readings apart, because B excludes A, so a coerced A can only reach f(A). The skeptic's citations are correct; the advanced chapter's two passages are the ones that settle it, and the repair's messages cite them. The value-dependent coerce in instruction 2 also proves the repair's cache reasoning: the coercions depend only on the argument types, which are the cache key (OverloadedFunction.java:760-776), but the type of a coerce result may depend on the value. So the declaration must be chosen per call on the converted arguments, never cached per the original types.

The worker is right on everything else checked:
- the recorded failure, committed before the edit (7aff60e42 before bedcb802d);
- the three shapes;
- the precedents, including the count of refusal sites, which the skeptic confirmed by a git grep;
- the placement at the team's TODO (OverloadedFunction.java:792 at the base);
- the ambiguity refusal;
- provisional rows 387-391;
- row 391's decision to have no gated test. Neither form of gated test would flip when the fix lands: a plain test expecting the compile to fail is red today, and an XXX test needs today's run to fail (FileTests.java:384-404, :577-583). The same holds for R1 and R2.

The skeptic is right on correction 2. The specification defines tuple coercion element by element (conversions-coercions.tex:667-705), and a variable declared with a tuple type is a typed binding, the third place of the rung's own decision (CLIMB-BATCH-4.md:59-63). The skeptic offered a repair or a home-2 test; I rule the repair. It is additive and confined to Coercions.coerce, and it reaches only a check that fails today.

Correction 3 is right. The worker's own probes/stay-green.txt:25-28 shows three changed tests. 'No interpreter test's output changed' is false as worded, however plainly nothing converts in them.

Correction 4 is right. mcache is declared at OverloadedMethod.java:28, read at :48 and :56, and never written, so the batch record's premise at CLIMB-BATCH-4.md:65 is also wrong.

Correction 5 is right. coerceToDeclared evaluates the declared type on every first force (Coercions.java:97-107, called from LazilyEvaluatedCell.java:66-68).

Correction 7 is right. CoercionMostSpecificRungC.fss:6, :10, :14 add excludes clauses, and without them :477-484 does not make ZZ64 more specific than ZZ128.

On correction 6, the skeptic gave SkStaticNarrow only a wording home. I rule that it takes home 2 as its own XXX file. It is a divergence that is measured, deferred and settled by the specification (:525-536, :567-570 give g(Wide)), and XXXCoercionStaticRungC asserts only the multiple-inheritance shape, so rule 2 of the three homes applies. Recommended rows R1-R5 are sound as written; I opened R1's type-annotation.tex:19-26 and R2's :633-638. Neither party's refusal points rest on the loud-to-quiet change of SkCycle, and that is right: walk has no checker, and the program's error is a static one.

